<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Generate Fantasy Names</h2>
    <p>Select a race or style, pick an optional gender lean, and choose how many names you want. Hit Generate and the tool produces a list instantly. Click any name in the results box to highlight it, or copy the full list at once using the button below.</p>
    <p>The generator runs entirely in your browser using a cryptographic random source, so results are genuinely unpredictable and the same settings rarely produce the same names twice.</p>
    <p>If you need a username for a game or social platform, the elf and human fantasy styles tend to produce usable combinations that are not already taken. Dark names work well for villain characters or edgy usernames.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>How Fantasy Name Generation Works</h2>
    <p>Each race style uses a distinct set of syllable patterns drawn from real linguistic traditions and fictional language designs. Elvish names borrow from melodic vowel-heavy constructions similar to Tolkien's Quenya. Dwarf names lean on hard consonants and shorter, punchy combinations. Orc names are guttural with more consonant clusters.</p>
    <p>The generator constructs names by combining prefixes, middle syllables, and suffixes according to that race's phonetic rules. The randomization uses window.crypto.getRandomValues, which is the same cryptographic API browsers use for security purposes. This ensures the output is statistically random with no repeated sequences.</p>
    <p>Adding a gender lean biases the syllable selection toward patterns that feel traditionally masculine or feminine in fantasy naming conventions. This is a soft preference, not a hard rule.</p>
</section>

